# TextField — label, input, hint, error, bound to the caller's cell. The facet
# this library said could not be written, and the note it could not be written in
# is still at the top of fieldlabel.fct: `bind` needs a state cell that exists at
# compile time, and a component parameter was not one, so `input bind cell` inside
# a component failed with *input binds unknown state "cell"* and the reusable unit
# of a form had to be the whole form.
#
# `v: cell text` is what changed. It declares that the caller passes the NAME of a
# `text` state cell, not its value; the compiler substitutes that name through the
# body and lowers a fresh copy per call site, so `input bind v` becomes
# `input bind draft` before anything is checked. Every check a hand-written input
# gets, this gets, against the real cell: that it exists, that it is `text` and not
# `int`, and that it is `@client` (two-way binding to an authoritative cell is
# still refused — the error names the cell the caller passed, not `v`).
#
#     state email: text = "" @client
#     use TextField("Email", "you@example.com", true, "We never share it.", failed(subscribe), email)
#
# THE PLACEHOLDER IS A REAL PARAMETER. It was left out of the first version of
# this facet because `placeholder` took a literal — `placeholder "{hint}"`
# compiled and rendered the eight characters `{hint}` into the box — and a
# parameter that renders its own source is worse than no parameter. The attribute
# interpolates now, and the value is escaped by both renderers, so nothing here
# has to sanitise it.
#
# One thing to know about interpolated attributes: outside a tracked region they
# render correctly but do not live-update when the value changes later. It does
# not bite here — a `use` is its own region, so the input and its placeholder are
# inside one — and it is the reason a placeholder driven by a cell that changes
# should be passed to a field rather than written onto a bare `input` at the top
# of a view.
#
# A placeholder is not a label and not a hint. It vanishes the moment there is a
# character in the box, so it can only ever show the SHAPE of the answer
# ("you@example.com", "+44 …"); the name of the field is `label`, and anything the
# reader still needs while typing is `hint`, which stays on screen.
#
# `NumberField` is the same body with `v: cell int`, and is a separate component
# rather than a `type` parameter because the cell's type is checked at the call
# site: one component cannot accept both, and a runtime "type" string would let a
# text cell into a number field.
import "field.fct"
app FacetForms:
    component TextField(label: text, placeholder: text, required: bool, hint: text, error: text, v: cell text):
        use Field(label, required, hint, error):
            input bind v placeholder "{placeholder}"
    component NumberField(label: text, placeholder: text, required: bool, hint: text, error: text, v: cell int):
        use Field(label, required, hint, error):
            input bind v placeholder "{placeholder}"
